<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Darłowo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rügenwalde</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Darłowo (German: Rügenwalde) is a small town on the Baltic coast in the West Pomeranian Voivodeship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, near the mouth of the Wieprza. Its castle was a residence of the Pomeranian dukes, and from 1449 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a>, deposed from his Scandinavian thrones, lived and ruled there until his death in 1459. He is buried in the town's Church of St Mary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darlowo.jpg" alt="Darłowo" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: VitVit · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dar%C5%82owo_radnice_a_kostel_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
